  "Question": "A basketball player made \\(5\\) baskets during a game. Each basket was worth either \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) points. How many different numbers could represent the total points scored by the player?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Five two-point baskets give \\(5\\cdot2=10\\) points. Replacing a two-point basket with a three-point basket increases the total by exactly \\(1\\).\n\nThe number of three-point baskets can be \\(0,1,2,3,4,\\) or \\(5\\), producing exactly the distinct totals\n\\[\n10,\\ 11,\\ 12,\\ 13,\\ 14,\\ 15.\n\\]\nEvery scoring pattern has one of these six numbers of three-point baskets, and each number is possible. Therefore, there are \\(\\boxed{6}\\) different totals, choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
